Mistake 1: Focusing on Absolute Numbers
Bad: "TLS adds 6 ms of latency"
Better: "TLS adds 50% overhead, but total latency is still under 20 ms"
Context matters. 6 ms is meaningless without knowing the baseline.

Mistake 2: Ignoring Variability
Bad: "Latency is 18 ms"
Better: "Latency averages 18 ms (min: 12 ms, max: 45 ms)"
Spikes can cause problems even when averages look good.

Mistake 3: Over-Generalizing
Bad: "TLS is slow" 
Better: "TLS adds approximately 50% overhead on my test system at 10 msg/sec"
Be specific about your conditions.

Mistake 4: Presenting Data Without Context
Bad: "Our system handles 50 messages per second with TLS enabled."
Better: "Our system handles 50 messages per second with TLS — meeting Grand Marina's emergency requirement. For comparison, published IoT security benchmarks show TLS typically adds 20-30% latency overhead on constrained devices, and our results fall within that range."
Your data becomes more convincing when you can show how it compares to industry benchmarks, real-world incidents, or compliance requirements. That's what separates a lab exercise from a professional assessment.
